WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.867
VGG.

2
00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:05.316
This 2014 network proved a simple design rule.

3
00:00:05.316 --> 00:00:09.767
It also teaches a lesson about where a model's size really hides.

4
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:13.867
VGG's recipe is wonderfully boring.

5
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:16.410
Don't invent exotic filter sizes.

6
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.333
Use only small three by three convolutions.

7
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.167
Stack lots of them, in a uniform pattern.

8
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.933
Each time the resolution halves, the number of channels doubles.

9
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.250
Why small filters?

10
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.800
Watch what stacking does.

11
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.700
One three by three convolution sees a three by three patch.

12
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.533
Stack a second, and together they see a five by five region.

13
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:39.549
A third sees seven by seven.

14
00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:44.633
That is the view of a big filter, but with fewer parameters and more non-linearity.

15
00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:47.258
Now the surprising part.

16
00:00:47.258 --> 00:00:49.633
Here is the parameter count in millions.

17
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:54.567
The convolutions everyone remembers hold only fourteen point seven million.

18
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:59.024
The three dense layers at the end hold one hundred twenty three million.

19
00:00:59.024 --> 00:01:01.333
That is nearly nine tenths of the model.

20
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:10.467
Modern networks replace that head with global average pooling, which is why ResNet-18 gets by with just eleven point seven million.

21
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.867
You can check this yourself in PyTorch.

22
00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:18.400
We write a tiny helper that sums the number of elements in every parameter.

23
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.192
Run it on VGG and the classifier head, and the head dominates.

24
00:01:23.192 --> 00:01:25.800
ResNet-18 is a fraction of the size.

25
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.267
Here's how this plays out in a real review.

26
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:36.933
A vendor proposes a VGG-16 classifier for your edge server, which has only two gigabytes of RAM.

27
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:42.600
You run the count: about five hundred twenty eight megabytes of weights, before activations.

28
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.910
You counter with ResNet-18 or EfficientNet.

29
00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:47.800
The numbers end the debate.

30
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:49.768
Let's recap.

31
00:01:49.768 --> 00:01:54.633
Stack uniform three by three convolutions, doubling channels as size halves.

32
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:58.133
Small stacks match big filters with fewer parameters.

33
00:01:58.433 --> 00:02:01.500
But the dense head holds most of VGG's weight.

34
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.282
So it lives on as a baseline, not a deployment choice.

35
00:02:05.282 --> 00:02:06.700
Next up: EfficientNet.
